  "parentCard": {
    "title": "The twenty-stones dawn walk",
    "minutes": 15,
    "includes": "why-walk",
    "steps": [
      "Head out early with a small bag and hunt stones together, counting each one out loud as it goes in: one, two, three.",
      "Stop at ten. Tip them out in a line, count the line together slowly, then push them into one pile and name it: that is a ten. It stays a ten now, and nobody counts it again.",
      "Keep hunting and start a second pile beside the first. Count on out loud as each new stone lands: ten and one, ten and two, ten and three.",
      "When the second pile reaches ten, stop and say it together: two whole tens, and that is twenty. Then count all twenty by ones, slowly, so they hear the two ways agree.",
      "On the way home let them pick something you walked past and wonder about it together, like why is this stone smooth and that one sharp? No right answer needed."
    ],
    "notice": [
      "Do they leave the first ten alone and count on from it, or start again at one every time a stone lands?",
      "Which comes out more easily, fourteen, or ten and four?",
      "At twenty, do they trust the two tens, or do they need to count all of them by ones before they believe it?"
    ]
  }
